{"name":"Trekking Blue","hex":"#4e606d","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"trekking","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["present participle and gerund of trek"]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Walking in the countryside for pleasure or sport, usually for a longer period of time than for hiking."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/trekking"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Backpacking (hiking)","blocks":[{"text":"Backpacking is the outdoor recreation practice of carrying gear on one's back while hiking for more than a day. It is often an extended journey and may involve camping outdoors. In North America, tenting is common, whereas simple shelters and mountain huts, widely found in Europe, are rare. In New Zealand, hiking is called tramping, and tents are used alongside a nationwide network of huts. Hill walking is equivalent in Britain, though backpackers make use of a variety of accommodation, in addition to camping. Backpackers use simple huts in South Africa. \"Trekking\" and \"bushwalking\" are other terms used to describe multi-day backpacking trips, with the term \"trekking\" emphasizing a more self-reliant approach to backpacking in more remote areas. The terms \"walking tour\" or \"long-distance hike\" are also used."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/Backpacking_in_polish_carpathians_cm01.jpg/330px-Backpacking_in_polish_carpathians_cm01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Backpacking_(hiking)"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Backpacking_in_Grand_Teton_NP-NPS.jpg/500px-Backpacking_in_Grand_Teton_NP-NPS.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Backpacking_in_Grand_Teton_NP-NPS.jpg/1280px-Backpacking_in_Grand_Teton_NP-NPS.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Backpacking_in_Grand_Teton_NP-NPS.jpg/500px-Backpacking_in_Grand_Teton_NP-NPS.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Backpacking in the Grand Teton National Park, Wyoming","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABackpacking_in_Grand_Teton_NP-NPS.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/Mountain_Jack_and_a_Wandering_Miner_.jpg/500px-Mountain_Jack_and_a_Wandering_Miner_.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/Mountain_Jack_and_a_Wandering_Miner_.jpg/1280px-Mountain_Jack_and_a_Wandering_Miner_.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/Mountain_Jack_and_a_Wandering_Miner_.jpg/500px-Mountain_Jack_and_a_Wandering_Miner_.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A miner carrying a backpack during the California Gold Rush","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMountain_Jack_and_a_Wandering_Miner_.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/Pocosin_cabin.jpg/500px-Pocosin_cabin.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/Pocosin_cabin.jpg/1280px-Pocosin_cabin.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/Pocosin_cabin.jpg/500px-Pocosin_cabin.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Pocosin cabin along the Appalachian Trail in Shenandoah National Park ","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APocosin_cabin.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Canteen_0002.jpg/500px-Canteen_0002.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Canteen_0002.jpg/1280px-Canteen_0002.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Canteen_0002.jpg/500px-Canteen_0002.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Military canteen with nested canteen cup and cover","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACanteen_0002.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/CampCooking.jpg/330px-CampCooki